{My most interesting game in the British Championship was against a fellow
Midlands competitor. It is easy to under­stand. Very early, I saddle D. G.
Horseman with a pawn weak­ness on the Queen’s side which is bound to prove
fatal unless he does something. This is the situation he likes, however, and
for some four hours he kept me so busy that I could not even begin to think
about the Queen’s side pawns. Finally he gets there and there is no more
than a draw for either player.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 b6 5. Nge2
Ba6 6. a3 Be7 7. Nf4 O-O 8. Be2 c5 9. dxc5 bxc5 10. Bf3 Nc6 11. Bxc6 dxc6 12.
b3 Qc7 13. O-O Rad8 14. Qc2 e5 15. Nfe2 Bc8 16. e4 Nh5 17. Be3 f5 18. f3 f4 19.
Bf2 Rf6 20. Na4 ({Too hurried: it was too early, even yet, to start attacking
those pawns. In analysis after the game, we found that} 20. Rfd1 {would have
been sounder.}) 20... Rg6 21. Kh1 {The king later comes all the way back to f1:
another advan­tage of 20.Rfd1 is that I could have played 21.Kf1 at once.} Rh6
22. Rfd1 Rf8 23. Rd3 Rff6 24. Kg1 Rfg6 25. Kf1 Nf6 26. Bg1 Rg5 27. Rad1 Rhg6
28. g3 fxg3 29. hxg3 Nh5 30. Bf2 Bh3+ 31. Ke1 Nxg3 32. Nxg3 Rxg3 33. Bxg3 Rxg3
34. Qh2 Bh4 35. Rd8+ Kf7 (35... Bxd8 36. Qxg3 {would suit White nicely.}) 36.
R8d7+ Bxd7 37. Qxh4 Rh3 38. Qf2 Rh1+ 39. Ke2 Rxd1 40. Kxd1 Qd6+ 41. Ke2 Qd4 42.
Nxc5 {Source: Birmingham Daily Post, 12 November 1957 - BH Wood column} 1/2-1/2

