1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bg5 dxc4 6. e4 b5 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5
9. Nxg5 hxg5 10. Bxg5 Nbd7 11. exf6 Bb7 12. Be2 $6 Qb6 13. O-O O-O-O 14. a4 b4
15. Ne4 c5 {... etc. No further moves available... "The best piece of
one-upmanship I have ever come across in chess occurred when I was playing
Adrian Hollis in 1957 in the British Boys' Chess Championship. After 15 moves
I had taken one hour on the clock and he had used up one minute. He leant over
to me and said "I suppose that you realise that we are following the game
Denker v Botvinnik?" I had the White pieces. "No," I replied, "who won?"
Adrian answered "Botvinnik." Like the innocent that I was I asked "when did
Denker make his mistake?" "Oh, he already had," was the devastating answer.
Naturally I lost the game shortly after." (Stewart Reuben, The Islingtonian No.
10 (1967))} (15... c5 16. Qb1 Qc7 17. Ng3 cxd4 18. Bxc4 Qc6 19. f3 d3 20. Qc1
Bc5+ 21. Kh1 Qd6 22. Qf4 Rxh2+ 23. Kxh2 Rh8+ 24. Qh4 Rxh4+ 25. Bxh4 Qf4 {
0-1, Denker-Botvinnik, USA v USSR Radio Match 1945}) 0-1

